1
00:00:13,362 --> 00:00:16,143
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is another Ajahn Chah talk.

2
00:00:16,223 --> 00:00:17,904
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just simply called Question and Answer.

3
00:00:20,745 --> 00:00:23,426
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a few questions from somebody.

4
00:00:23,766 --> 00:00:24,446
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know who that is.

5
00:00:24,826 --> 00:00:25,906
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, Ajahn Chah answers.

6
00:00:27,907 --> 00:00:32,249
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question... question... first question is...

7
00:00:32,289 --> 00:00:41,552
[SPEAKER_00]: There are those periods when our hearts or mind happen to be absorbed in things and become blemished or darkened, but we are still aware of ourselves.

8
00:00:42,113 --> 00:00:45,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Such as when some form of greed, hatred and delusion comes up.

9
00:00:47,096 --> 00:00:52,578
[SPEAKER_00]: Although we know that these things are objectionable, we are unable to prevent them from arising.

10
00:00:53,879 --> 00:01:05,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Could it be said that even as we are aware of them, this is providing the basis for increased clinging and attachment, and maybe is putting us further back to where we started.

11
00:01:06,868 --> 00:01:07,709
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you say that again?

12
00:01:08,149 --> 00:01:17,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Could we say... Could it be said that even as we are aware of them, this is providing the basis for increased clinging and attachment?

13
00:01:18,259 --> 00:01:18,599
[SPEAKER_01]: What is?

14
00:01:18,779 --> 00:01:20,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Their awareness of it or the thing in itself?

15
00:01:21,262 --> 00:01:22,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Being aware of it.

16
00:01:22,483 --> 00:01:24,284
[SPEAKER_01]: That's providing basis for further clinging.

17
00:01:24,364 --> 00:01:24,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

18
00:01:24,805 --> 00:01:24,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

19
00:01:25,265 --> 00:01:28,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe it's putting us further back to where we started from.

20
00:01:29,029 --> 00:01:29,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

21
00:01:30,130 --> 00:01:32,392
[SPEAKER_00]: But Ajahn Chah says... Well...

22
00:01:33,063 --> 00:01:34,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you read again the question from the start?

23
00:01:34,944 --> 00:01:37,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me say it first and then tell me what Ajahn Chah says.

24
00:01:41,149 --> 00:01:42,630
[SPEAKER_00]: So the question begins.

25
00:01:43,330 --> 00:01:55,298
[SPEAKER_00]: There are those periods when our minds happen to be absorbed in things and become blemished or darkened, but we are still aware of ourselves, such as when some form of greed, hatred and delusion comes up.

26
00:01:56,299 --> 00:01:58,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Although we know that these things are

27
00:01:58,813 --> 00:01:59,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Objectionable.

28
00:02:00,034 --> 00:02:02,095
[SPEAKER_00]: We are unable to prevent them from arising.

29
00:02:02,135 --> 00:02:03,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's it.

30
00:02:04,116 --> 00:02:05,357
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the wrong assumption there.

31
00:02:06,578 --> 00:02:13,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to prevent these things from arising, which means not seeing where your responsibility is, but overdoing it.

32
00:02:13,122 --> 00:02:16,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to get rid of... trying to prevent things from manifesting in the first place.

33
00:02:17,204 --> 00:02:18,085
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not on you.

34
00:02:18,525 --> 00:02:19,365
[SPEAKER_01]: That cannot be on you.

35
00:02:20,126 --> 00:02:28,351
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the response... you're... and that's why they would get confused then, and... and end up revolving around the objectionable states.

36
00:02:29,837 --> 00:02:35,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they're overdoing the aspect of trying to get rid of it, and that's how you bind yourself with it.

37
00:02:35,879 --> 00:02:39,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a dog that runs around the post, trying to get away from the post.

38
00:02:39,701 --> 00:02:40,901
[SPEAKER_01]: It's tied to the post.

39
00:02:41,001 --> 00:02:44,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Tries to get away from the post, ends up being tighter and tighter, closer to the post.

40
00:02:46,063 --> 00:02:48,644
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if that person were to recognize that they can't...

41
00:02:49,605 --> 00:02:50,365
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not up to you.

42
00:02:51,133 --> 00:02:55,897
[SPEAKER_01]: To prevent things from manifesting or not, that's not your responsibility, and that's not the problem either.

43
00:02:56,337 --> 00:03:00,480
[SPEAKER_01]: But usually people believe, oh, there is greed in my mind, I must get rid of it.

44
00:03:01,041 --> 00:03:02,302
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not the problem.

45
00:03:02,722 --> 00:03:08,507
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is you acting out of the greed, out of aversion, and that's on you.

46
00:03:09,207 --> 00:03:18,775
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to be able to not interfere with thing that has manifested on its own, and then recognize that you're not responsible for that, and that's not the issue, and that's not the greed in itself.

47
00:03:19,598 --> 00:03:21,560
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the prospect of greed being presented.

48
00:03:22,520 --> 00:03:28,926
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's you then choosing to act by body, speech or mind, delighting it, by with mental... delighting in it, welcoming it.

49
00:03:29,306 --> 00:03:30,027
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your doing.

50
00:03:30,787 --> 00:03:37,573
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the Buddha says you shouldn't delight in greed, aversion, delusion, means that you don't delight in what has presented itself.

51
00:03:38,634 --> 00:03:42,237
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean I must get rid of what has... because that would mean that you are in control.

52
00:03:42,937 --> 00:03:44,879
[SPEAKER_01]: That you are... that you do have a say.

53
00:03:46,032 --> 00:03:47,512
[SPEAKER_01]: As to what appears, what doesn't.

54
00:03:48,133 --> 00:03:52,214
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that if you were to truly have a say, these things wouldn't have arisen in the first place.

55
00:03:52,994 --> 00:03:56,755
[SPEAKER_01]: But they do arise on their own, and you have no say, yet you go around acting like you have a say.

56
00:03:57,175 --> 00:03:57,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

57
00:03:57,395 --> 00:03:59,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't know where the problem is.

58
00:04:00,116 --> 00:04:06,918
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you think the problem is in manifestation, and not in the attitude, your attitude towards the manifestation.

59
00:04:07,598 --> 00:04:09,459
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's where the restraint comes first.

60
00:04:09,499 --> 00:04:10,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Not to prevent things from arising,

61
00:04:11,778 --> 00:04:20,760
[SPEAKER_01]: But to enable you to allow things to arise without acting out of... towards them, without acting out of it, out of that pressure of arising.

62
00:04:21,780 --> 00:04:27,641
[SPEAKER_01]: And the more restrained you are, means the less interfering with things manifestation you would be doing.

63
00:04:30,381 --> 00:04:33,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Anyway, carry on.

64
00:04:33,982 --> 00:04:36,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Anyway, Ajahn Chah answers that.

65
00:04:36,542 --> 00:04:37,622
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, that's it.

66
00:04:37,742 --> 00:04:39,623
[SPEAKER_00]: One must keep knowing them at that point.

67
00:04:39,943 --> 00:04:41,183
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the method of practice.

68
00:04:42,224 --> 00:04:43,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing them and the point of their arising.

69
00:04:44,265 --> 00:04:45,786
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

70
00:04:46,087 --> 00:04:54,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Then the questioner asks, I mean that simultaneously we are both aware of them and repelled by them, but lacking the ability to resist them.

71
00:04:55,092 --> 00:04:59,415
[SPEAKER_00]: They just burst forth.

72
00:04:59,475 --> 00:05:02,417
[SPEAKER_01]: They can't burst forth unless you act, you choose to act out of them.

73
00:05:02,877 --> 00:05:03,818
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what they don't see.

74
00:05:03,838 --> 00:05:04,678
[SPEAKER_00]: You push them forward.

75
00:05:04,698 --> 00:05:04,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

76
00:05:05,499 --> 00:05:07,160
[SPEAKER_01]: The pressure might be, yeah, sure.

77
00:05:07,700 --> 00:05:11,603
[SPEAKER_01]: But no, no amount of the pressure can sort of

78
00:05:12,349 --> 00:05:13,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Make a choice for you.

79
00:05:14,151 --> 00:05:26,000
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always you who chooses to act out of the pressure, which means you can also choose not to act, which means you are the doorway through which the pressure can enter, because you are the one who opens the door, or doesn't.

80
00:05:28,242 --> 00:05:39,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing that, then, yeah, it feels like as if the action came automatically out of the pressure, because you haven't practiced restraint sufficiently enough, properly enough, so you haven't discerned that line

81
00:05:40,524 --> 00:05:44,845
[SPEAKER_01]: For which you're responsible, after which, like, you don't cross.

82
00:05:46,926 --> 00:05:53,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Ajahn Chah answers, By then, it's already beyond one's capability to do anything.

83
00:05:54,108 --> 00:05:57,829
[SPEAKER_00]: At that point, one has to readjust oneself and then continue contemplation.

84
00:05:58,409 --> 00:06:00,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't just give up on them, there and then.

85
00:06:00,930 --> 00:06:05,191
[SPEAKER_00]: When one sees things arise in that way, one tends to get upset or feel regret.

86
00:06:05,972 --> 00:06:10,233
[SPEAKER_00]: But it is possible to say that they are uncertain and subject to change.

87
00:06:11,945 --> 00:06:26,109
[SPEAKER_00]: What happens is that one sees these things are wrong, but one is still not ready to or able to deal with them, as if they are independent entities, the leftover karmic tendencies that are still creating and conditioning the state of the mind.

88
00:06:27,749 --> 00:06:37,992
[SPEAKER_00]: One doesn't wish to allow the mind to become like that, but it does, and it indicates that one's knowledge and awareness is still neither sufficient nor fast enough to keep abreast of things.

89
00:06:39,407 --> 00:06:45,512
[SPEAKER_00]: One must practice and develop mindfulness as much as one can in order to gain a greater and more penetrating awareness.

90
00:06:46,893 --> 00:06:50,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether the mind is soiled or blemished in some way, it doesn't matter.

91
00:06:51,476 --> 00:06:55,660
[SPEAKER_00]: What comes up, one should contemplate the impermanence and uncertainty of it.

92
00:06:56,841 --> 00:07:07,729
[SPEAKER_00]: By maintaining this contemplation at each instant that something arises, after some time, one will see the impermanent nature inherent in all sense objects and mental states.

93
00:07:10,465 --> 00:07:20,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Because one sees them as such, gradually they will lose their importance and one's clinging and attachment to that, which is a blemish on the mind, will continue to diminish.

94
00:07:22,028 --> 00:07:26,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever suffering arises, one will be able to work through it and readjust oneself.

95
00:07:27,110 --> 00:07:30,691
[SPEAKER_00]: But one shouldn't give up on this work or set it aside.

96
00:07:31,211 --> 00:07:38,193
[SPEAKER_00]: One must keep up continuity of effort and try to make one's awareness fast enough to keep in touch with the changing mental conditions.

97
00:07:40,867 --> 00:07:51,411
[SPEAKER_00]: It could be said that so far one's development of the path still lacks sufficient energy to overcome the mental defilements.

98
00:07:52,171 --> 00:08:01,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever suffering arises, the mind becomes clouded over, and one must keep developing that knowledge and understanding of that clouded mind.

99
00:08:02,195 --> 00:08:03,595
[SPEAKER_00]: That is what one reflects on.

100
00:08:06,968 --> 00:08:13,850
[SPEAKER_00]: One must really take hold of it and repeatedly contemplate that this suffering and discontentment is not just a sure thing.

101
00:08:14,591 --> 00:08:18,652
[SPEAKER_00]: It's something that is ultimately impermanent, unsatisfactory in itself.

102
00:08:19,892 --> 00:08:28,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Focusing on these three characteristics, whenever these conditions of suffering arise, again, one will know them straight away, having experienced them before.

103
00:08:28,996 --> 00:08:33,117
[SPEAKER_00]: And gradually, little by little, one's practice should gain momentum, and as time passes,

104
00:08:34,097 --> 00:08:39,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever sense, objects and mental state arise, will lose their value in this way.

105
00:08:40,459 --> 00:08:55,605
[SPEAKER_00]: One's heart will know them for what they are and accordingly put them down, having reached the point where one is able to know things and put them down with ease.

106
00:08:57,185 --> 00:09:02,367
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he didn't actually, well, he didn't actually provide a direct answer to the question.

107
00:09:04,495 --> 00:09:04,895
[SPEAKER_01]: It was...

108
00:09:04,915 --> 00:09:21,561
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it's fine, but you could see he was talking around, as in, well, you just have to keep practicing, which is true, but you could have answered it more accurately and point out to a person to start... Like, you can say, oh, you practice and you'll develop wisdom.

109
00:09:21,781 --> 00:09:22,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

110
00:09:22,621 --> 00:09:25,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can say, well, look for these things and you'll develop wisdom.

111
00:09:26,582 --> 00:09:34,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Things such as recognize experience of greed, aversion, delusion in its manifestation.

112
00:09:35,492 --> 00:09:36,992
[SPEAKER_01]: So, don't try to negate that.

113
00:09:37,492 --> 00:09:38,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't try to get rid of it.

114
00:09:40,253 --> 00:09:43,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Because by that, by doing that, you're implying the sense of control.

115
00:09:43,654 --> 00:09:50,955
[SPEAKER_01]: You're implying the ownership, the master, you know, this will, this should not be existing.

116
00:09:51,115 --> 00:09:59,897
[SPEAKER_01]: But instead, discern thing enduring in itself, and that's how your attitude towards it will become more apparent.

117
00:10:01,097 --> 00:10:04,498
[SPEAKER_01]: So, Ajahn Chah's answer is obviously, you know, there's nothing wrong with it.

118
00:10:05,391 --> 00:10:08,692
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's quite a roundabout way of just sort of telling people, well, just keep doing it.

119
00:10:08,812 --> 00:10:10,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Telling that person, just keep doing it.

120
00:10:10,133 --> 00:10:11,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's true, just keep doing it.

121
00:10:12,053 --> 00:10:18,495
[SPEAKER_01]: But more, more accurately, more acutely, stop trying to get rid of things that are not your responsibility.

122
00:10:18,635 --> 00:10:20,496
[SPEAKER_01]: And rākuṇa is where responsibility is.

123
00:10:20,956 --> 00:10:21,716
[SPEAKER_00]: He gives a simile.

124
00:10:23,137 --> 00:10:23,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's go over it.

125
00:10:24,797 --> 00:10:29,779
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, from then on, there will just be arising a passing away in this place.

126
00:10:31,103 --> 00:10:32,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

127
00:10:32,043 --> 00:10:34,945
[SPEAKER_00]: The same as waves striking the seashore.

128
00:10:35,125 --> 00:10:40,307
[SPEAKER_00]: When a wave comes in and finally reaches the shoreline, it just disintegrates and vanishes.

129
00:10:40,827 --> 00:10:44,188
[SPEAKER_00]: A new wave comes and it happens again.

130
00:10:44,609 --> 00:10:47,590
[SPEAKER_00]: The wave going no further than the limit of the shoreline.

131
00:10:48,510 --> 00:10:53,172
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same way, nothing will be able to go beyond the limits established by one's own awareness.

132
00:10:54,052 --> 00:10:54,413
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

133
00:10:54,493 --> 00:10:55,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing can enter through that door.

134
00:10:56,734 --> 00:10:59,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you realize nothing could have ever entered.

135
00:10:59,635 --> 00:11:00,035
[SPEAKER_01]: It's you.

136
00:11:00,572 --> 00:11:03,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Who is going out through the door and being affected by things that are outside.

137
00:11:04,174 --> 00:11:09,256
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it was you who was stepping from the shore into the water that's being hit by the waves.

138
00:11:10,957 --> 00:11:11,938
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly what I'm talking about.

139
00:11:12,398 --> 00:11:16,580
[SPEAKER_01]: You start discerning where your responsibility is and then you don't cross it.

140
00:11:17,601 --> 00:11:22,663
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize the mind can arise in all shapes and forms, whatever it wants, whichever way it wants.

141
00:11:24,384 --> 00:11:25,184
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the problem.

142
00:11:25,324 --> 00:11:26,385
[SPEAKER_01]: There never was a problem.

143
00:11:28,415 --> 00:11:31,096
[SPEAKER_01]: You're welcoming it, entertaining it, trying to get rid of it.

144
00:11:31,456 --> 00:11:32,317
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the problem is.

145
00:11:32,357 --> 00:11:39,319
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stop doing that, means that which used to be a basis for acts of greed, still arises.

146
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:43,321
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is no greed, because greed comes from you, doesn't come from things.

147
00:11:44,782 --> 00:11:47,743
[SPEAKER_01]: That which used to be basis for ill-will, still arises.

148
00:11:49,223 --> 00:11:51,284
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is no ill-will now, in regard to it.

149
00:11:52,725 --> 00:11:57,887
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the waves still continue to come and go, but you don't get wet anymore.

150
00:11:58,332 --> 00:12:00,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't go outside where the waves are.

151
00:12:00,413 --> 00:12:00,994
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize.

152
00:12:01,594 --> 00:12:09,519
[SPEAKER_01]: And people start going outside, or they carry on going outside, where you don't recognize where the safety from the wave is as well.

153
00:12:10,420 --> 00:12:12,982
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you think I must prevent waves from coming.

154
00:12:13,062 --> 00:12:15,463
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the only way to remain dry.

155
00:12:15,764 --> 00:12:16,244
[SPEAKER_01]: But it isn't.

156
00:12:16,704 --> 00:12:22,268
[SPEAKER_01]: You just have to see the range of the wave and allow it to basically express itself, so to speak.

157
00:12:23,260 --> 00:12:23,760
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

158
00:12:23,780 --> 00:12:25,921
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to recognize things in their manifestation.

159
00:12:25,941 --> 00:12:27,182
[SPEAKER_01]: They have manifested on its own.

160
00:12:27,242 --> 00:12:28,523
[SPEAKER_01]: The wave has come on its own.

161
00:12:30,164 --> 00:12:32,785
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's you who goes there and tries to push the sea back.

162
00:12:34,466 --> 00:12:37,487
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when you... That's... Basically, that's what person's question implied.

163
00:12:37,948 --> 00:12:42,270
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I... How do I, like, deal with this from arising, so it doesn't arise?

164
00:12:42,310 --> 00:12:48,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Because for him, automatically coming of the waves means automatically me walking into the water and getting wet.

165
00:12:49,200 --> 00:12:51,261
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't see the difference between the two things.

166
00:12:51,281 --> 00:12:52,081
[SPEAKER_01]: It means flood.

167
00:12:52,261 --> 00:12:52,861
[SPEAKER_01]: It means flood.

168
00:12:52,881 --> 00:12:53,322
[SPEAKER_01]: It means what?

169
00:12:53,342 --> 00:12:54,722
[SPEAKER_01]: It means soaked, drenched.

170
00:12:56,443 --> 00:12:57,543
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to recognize... No, no, no.

171
00:12:58,023 --> 00:13:00,985
[SPEAKER_01]: The wave can come as like tsunami wave.

172
00:13:01,845 --> 00:13:04,426
[SPEAKER_01]: It still can't cross this line unless I cross it that way.

173
00:13:06,107 --> 00:13:06,607
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I mean.

174
00:13:06,627 --> 00:13:09,368
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where sensory strain will enable you to start discerning that.

175
00:13:10,348 --> 00:13:17,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Without sensory strain, yeah, wave means I'm down in the water, either swimming with it or trying to go against it.

176
00:13:19,524 --> 00:13:20,985
[SPEAKER_01]: So, actually, the simile is quite accurate.

177
00:13:22,286 --> 00:13:23,187
[SPEAKER_01]: The simile is quite good.

178
00:13:24,188 --> 00:13:31,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Because once you start discerning that, it's like finding that a single doorway through which things come in because you invite them in.

179
00:13:32,035 --> 00:13:34,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Or that island that no flood can overwhelm.

180
00:13:34,297 --> 00:13:35,718
[SPEAKER_01]: The island that no flood can overwhelm.

181
00:13:35,918 --> 00:13:41,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Or as the Buddha gave the other one, the town that's surrounded by the wall and there is only one point of entry and exit.

182
00:13:41,603 --> 00:13:45,066
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, well, if I want to keep track of what's going on inside,

183
00:13:46,325 --> 00:13:49,326
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to chase people who come in and go after people they go out.

184
00:13:49,366 --> 00:13:51,106
[SPEAKER_01]: I just need to stay at the doorway.

185
00:13:52,927 --> 00:14:00,068
[SPEAKER_01]: The doorway of that mindful context, in regard to feeling, in regard to perceptions, in regard to intentions.

186
00:14:00,088 --> 00:14:03,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the doorway of focusing on the tip of your nose where you're not aware of it.

187
00:14:03,469 --> 00:14:10,171
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically like you staring at the actual doorway and you don't see who walks through the doorway, because you're staring at the woodwork of it.

188
00:14:11,551 --> 00:14:16,112
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you want to see, okay, whoever comes in, I know that it's in, because there's no other way of going out.

189
00:14:17,105 --> 00:14:25,228
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever manifests itself, you know it manifested to this extent, and there is lust in the mind in regard to it, or there is non-lust in regard to it.

190
00:14:25,348 --> 00:14:27,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Neither of those things are still a problem.

191
00:14:27,829 --> 00:14:37,253
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is when you start entertaining that lust, and then welcoming it, welcoming the prospect of what that lust is pointing at, and that's how you start acting out of the desire.

192
00:14:39,994 --> 00:14:44,997
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't act out of desire and ill-will, those things cannot enter your mind.

193
00:14:45,017 --> 00:14:46,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Even when you actually act,

194
00:14:46,636 --> 00:14:47,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Those things can't enter your mind.

195
00:14:48,677 --> 00:14:51,558
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why freedom from those things is possible in the first place.

196
00:14:51,618 --> 00:14:53,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Choice is not mine.

197
00:14:53,398 --> 00:14:54,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Choice is not mine.

198
00:14:54,759 --> 00:14:54,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

199
00:14:55,179 --> 00:15:03,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, see, people... You can eventually work your way up, whereby you see the actual present choice as not being able to be owned by you.

200
00:15:04,222 --> 00:15:10,684
[SPEAKER_01]: But until you work your way up in this gradual manner, all you're going to be doing is denying the choice after you made it.

201
00:15:11,985 --> 00:15:13,185
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a form of determinism.

202
00:15:13,933 --> 00:15:17,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you made the choice, but then you say, well, there's no self, so this choice couldn't have been mine.

203
00:15:19,257 --> 00:15:20,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it was yours when you were making it.

204
00:15:20,978 --> 00:15:21,618
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you know that?

205
00:15:21,778 --> 00:15:24,901
[SPEAKER_01]: It was rooted in greed, or aversion, or distraction and delusion.

206
00:15:25,661 --> 00:15:31,305
[SPEAKER_01]: And whenever there is any of these three, there is the conceit, there is sense of I am, which means it is your choice.

207
00:15:32,686 --> 00:15:42,133
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the better way to go about it, not to kind of deny it in hindsight, but actually take it as a blanket rule, that the choice is always yours.

208
00:15:43,333 --> 00:15:54,795
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through that sense of responsibility for the slightest choice you make, you can then eventually overcome the ownership of the choice and responsibility with it, not remove the responsibility for the choice.

209
00:15:55,736 --> 00:16:00,737
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in order to... in order to remove the ownership of responsibility, you have to stop denying responsibility.

210
00:16:01,677 --> 00:16:05,618
[SPEAKER_01]: And people deny responsibility when they say, well, choice is not mine, nothing is mine.

211
00:16:07,738 --> 00:16:10,478
[SPEAKER_01]: Or people try to deny responsibility when they try to prevent things.

212
00:16:11,419 --> 00:16:12,819
[SPEAKER_01]: They were basis for the choices.

213
00:16:13,883 --> 00:16:16,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the question implied.

214
00:16:16,945 --> 00:16:28,154
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as Ajahn Chah pointed out, you have to stop... Well, you have to stop engaging with things on that level, but start discerning where you're doing is, and then stop doing it.

215
00:16:28,234 --> 00:16:31,496
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's that safe shore you find, that it's inaccessible to these waves.

216
00:16:32,016 --> 00:16:39,442
[SPEAKER_01]: So, these waves remain right in front of you, coming your way at full force, but now you have confidence that they can't cross, because you know that they can't cross.

217
00:16:40,975 --> 00:16:43,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you stayed with it long enough and you saw that they can't cross.

218
00:16:45,599 --> 00:16:48,782
[SPEAKER_01]: If you haven't stayed with it long enough, well, you still doubt whether they can cross or not.

219
00:16:49,903 --> 00:16:51,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, you were affected by it.

220
00:16:53,286 --> 00:16:54,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you don't feel safe.

221
00:16:54,647 --> 00:16:56,769
[SPEAKER_01]: If you doubt your safety, you can't say you're safe.

222
00:17:00,652 --> 00:17:07,698
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to stop doubting your safety is to discern what that safety is in regard to these things that are assailing you.

223
00:17:09,392 --> 00:17:13,855
[SPEAKER_01]: But the natural reaction is, I must prevent these things from assailing me in the first place.

224
00:17:13,895 --> 00:17:14,936
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the true safety.

225
00:17:15,737 --> 00:17:16,077
[SPEAKER_01]: It isn't.

226
00:17:17,178 --> 00:17:17,978
[SPEAKER_00]: I must destroy them.

227
00:17:18,239 --> 00:17:19,099
[SPEAKER_01]: I must destroy them.

228
00:17:19,179 --> 00:17:19,960
[SPEAKER_01]: I must...

229
00:17:19,980 --> 00:17:20,861
[SPEAKER_01]: I must remove them.

230
00:17:21,001 --> 00:17:22,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Rather than get to safety.

231
00:17:22,582 --> 00:17:22,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

232
00:17:23,062 --> 00:17:23,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Rather than actually...

233
00:17:23,863 --> 00:17:24,243
[SPEAKER_01]: Hold on.

234
00:17:25,364 --> 00:17:28,266
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't be fighting the sea and the waves and the elements.

235
00:17:28,887 --> 00:17:32,950
[SPEAKER_01]: All I can do is find a place where those elements can't... basically can't set their foot in.

236
00:17:35,692 --> 00:17:37,253
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when nibbāna is that negative.

237
00:17:42,183 --> 00:17:43,424
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to maintain it.

238
00:17:43,624 --> 00:17:44,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course not.

239
00:17:44,624 --> 00:17:45,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible to maintain it.

240
00:17:47,205 --> 00:17:47,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

241
00:17:47,765 --> 00:17:50,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Because maintenance is on the level of intention, choice.

242
00:17:50,986 --> 00:17:53,286
[SPEAKER_01]: And this is safe in regard to...

243
00:17:53,767 --> 00:17:54,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Remember it?

244
00:17:54,807 --> 00:17:54,947
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

245
00:17:55,227 --> 00:17:57,648
[SPEAKER_01]: You discern it in regard to the threat.

246
00:17:58,848 --> 00:18:00,288
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it requires no maintenance.

247
00:18:00,889 --> 00:18:03,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Because any manifestation is a threat.

248
00:18:05,310 --> 00:18:09,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means safety is in regard to any threat.

249
00:18:09,822 --> 00:18:11,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means it's in regard to any manifestation.

250
00:18:12,343 --> 00:18:20,968
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the independent manifestation of thing, which is not your doing, that maintains your sense of safety, which is equally not your doing.

251
00:18:21,448 --> 00:18:24,970
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why arahantship is effortless, once you develop it.

252
00:18:25,190 --> 00:18:27,011
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you only need manifestation.

253
00:18:27,171 --> 00:18:39,538
[SPEAKER_01]: You only need to work, initially, to stop obscuring the true nature of manifestations, that it cannot be owned, because of that it's impermanent, that it's unsatisfactory, because it doesn't have that

254
00:18:42,299 --> 00:18:44,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense of pleasure connected with mastery over things.

255
00:18:45,420 --> 00:18:46,841
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, it cannot be yours.

256
00:18:47,782 --> 00:18:48,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, it's not self.

257
00:18:49,662 --> 00:19:00,768
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always... then that manifestation, discerning a thing, it reveals all the time that safe zone.

258
00:19:01,028 --> 00:19:01,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

259
00:19:01,428 --> 00:19:02,108
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the thing.

260
00:19:02,608 --> 00:19:05,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you fully understood it, you don't have to keep discerning it.

261
00:19:05,430 --> 00:19:05,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

262
00:19:06,348 --> 00:19:17,191
[SPEAKER_01]: The only... like an ārya, sāvaka, needs to keep discerning it, because he still has residues of habits of ignorance and lust and so on.

263
00:19:17,211 --> 00:19:22,812
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he has to keep going against that grain until it's fully purified and then there is nothing with the grain anymore.

264
00:19:26,053 --> 00:19:31,995
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just saying this so people don't develop... you might... like Arahant is just constantly, this is a manifestation, this is a manifestation... No.

265
00:19:32,802 --> 00:19:35,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you're aware of, it has been manifested.

266
00:19:35,342 --> 00:19:37,143
[SPEAKER_01]: To that extent, you're fully aware of it already.

267
00:19:37,823 --> 00:19:39,943
[SPEAKER_01]: And to that extent, you're fully free from it already.

268
00:19:40,283 --> 00:19:42,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there was nothing to affect you in the first place.

269
00:19:43,724 --> 00:19:48,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's think, well, and Aryan, then, would... You're aware of it...

270
00:19:50,165 --> 00:19:51,485
[SPEAKER_00]: It reveals the island.

271
00:19:52,285 --> 00:19:52,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

272
00:19:52,885 --> 00:19:53,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

273
00:19:53,445 --> 00:19:55,586
[SPEAKER_00]: The island is revealed by the waves.

274
00:19:56,286 --> 00:19:56,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

275
00:19:57,626 --> 00:19:58,166
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all you need is...

276
00:19:58,186 --> 00:20:00,927
[SPEAKER_01]: The island is outlined by the sea.

277
00:20:00,947 --> 00:20:01,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

278
00:20:02,207 --> 00:20:02,447
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

279
00:20:04,634 --> 00:20:10,078
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the unborn, like if the sea is born, nibbāna is the unborn, the unmanifested.

280
00:20:10,438 --> 00:20:17,544
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from whatever is manifested, nibbāna is outlined by being the opposite of that.

281
00:20:17,624 --> 00:20:21,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Undefined, undesignated, undetermined.

282
00:20:23,869 --> 00:20:27,231
[SPEAKER_00]: And by recognizing that, you can't unrecognize it.

283
00:20:27,391 --> 00:20:27,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

284
00:20:28,232 --> 00:20:31,254
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't go back the other way and pretend you haven't recognized it.

285
00:20:31,375 --> 00:20:33,236
[SPEAKER_00]: You haven't recognized the extent of manifestation.

286
00:20:33,788 --> 00:20:34,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yeah.

287
00:20:34,749 --> 00:20:36,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that is the true nature of manifestations.

288
00:20:37,970 --> 00:20:38,831
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot be owned.

289
00:20:39,812 --> 00:20:41,833
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot be pleasurable.

290
00:20:41,893 --> 00:20:43,394
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you cannot be your own.

291
00:20:45,595 --> 00:20:46,595
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the true nature.

292
00:20:47,596 --> 00:20:54,260
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why liberation from the ownership, this sense of self and the dependence on pleasure is possible.

293
00:20:54,640 --> 00:20:57,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you actually get to discern and then these things become inconceivable.

294
00:20:58,162 --> 00:20:59,343
[SPEAKER_01]: If those things were not truly

295
00:21:02,102 --> 00:21:08,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Beyond the ownership, unpleasant, not your sense of self, they can't be maintained in regard to it.

296
00:21:08,103 --> 00:21:10,643
[SPEAKER_01]: If they were not truly like that, you wouldn't be able to free yourself from it.

297
00:21:11,624 --> 00:21:14,344
[SPEAKER_01]: The sense of self would be the master and owner of the experience.

298
00:21:14,864 --> 00:21:17,045
[SPEAKER_01]: But that means there wouldn't be any problems or suffering.

299
00:21:17,285 --> 00:21:21,665
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that there is a problem of suffering, means the sense of ownership is incompatible with that.

300
00:21:22,906 --> 00:21:28,407
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why human existence is a discrepancy between how things actually are and how you're acting them to be.

301
00:21:29,367 --> 00:21:30,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why you're responsible for their being.

302
00:21:33,752 --> 00:21:43,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Discrepancy, dukkha... Any degree of discrepancy determines the degree of liability towards the dukkha.

303
00:21:45,337 --> 00:21:47,878
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no discrepancy, no base for dukkha.

304
00:21:48,118 --> 00:21:48,758
[SPEAKER_01]: That's arahant.

305
00:21:50,519 --> 00:21:51,939
[SPEAKER_01]: As in the scene, there's just the scene.

306
00:21:53,220 --> 00:21:54,020
[SPEAKER_01]: A is just A.

307
00:21:54,120 --> 00:21:55,100
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no discrepancy there.

308
00:21:56,161 --> 00:22:00,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, the people sometimes get the sense of it, but then they look for the discrepancy at the wrong place.

309
00:22:00,202 --> 00:22:01,763
[SPEAKER_01]: They look at the discrepancy in the A.

310
00:22:02,663 --> 00:22:05,584
[SPEAKER_01]: And they try to resolve A of discrepancy.

311
00:22:06,224 --> 00:22:11,045
[SPEAKER_01]: The scene must be resolved of the lust, and aversion, delusion, of the discrepancy of it.

312
00:22:11,105 --> 00:22:11,405
[SPEAKER_01]: But no.

313
00:22:12,025 --> 00:22:14,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Lust, and aversion, delusion is not in the scene.

314
00:22:15,506 --> 00:22:16,667
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in your attitude towards it.

315
00:22:17,307 --> 00:22:19,927
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where you remove that kind of attitude.

316
00:22:19,947 --> 00:22:21,028
[SPEAKER_01]: You undo it.

317
00:22:21,628 --> 00:22:22,548
[SPEAKER_01]: You extinguish it.

318
00:22:23,708 --> 00:22:28,169
[SPEAKER_01]: And then what you have is in the scene, there's just the scene.

319
00:22:29,170 --> 00:22:30,450
[SPEAKER_01]: In the herd, there's just the herd.

320
00:22:33,112 --> 00:22:34,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Smell, taste, touch, same.

321
00:22:34,772 --> 00:22:35,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Thought, thought.

322
00:22:38,053 --> 00:22:43,235
[SPEAKER_01]: But what person's question implied is, oh, there is a scene of greed, aversion, delusion.

323
00:22:43,275 --> 00:22:46,636
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I prevent it from being seen?

324
00:22:46,656 --> 00:22:53,478
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he didn't see, he doesn't see that the issue was in his attitude towards the scene, not the scene.

325
00:22:54,398 --> 00:23:02,220
[SPEAKER_01]: So, his question of trying to deal with the scene, prevent it, make it not, make it unmanifest,

326
00:23:04,028 --> 00:23:13,815
[SPEAKER_00]: Here is another Ajahn Chah talk called Clarity of Insight.

327
00:23:14,135 --> 00:23:16,457
[SPEAKER_00]: There's just one paragraph from that talk.

328
00:23:22,421 --> 00:23:30,566
[SPEAKER_00]: So he says, Ajahn Chah says, the investigation and development of insight into the dhamma gives rise to this profound peace of mind.

329
00:23:31,948 --> 00:23:45,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Having gained such clear and penetrating insight means it is sustained at all times, whether you are sitting meditation, with your eyes closed, or even if you are doing something with your eyes open.

330
00:23:45,173 --> 00:23:51,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever situation you find yourself in, be it in formal meditation or not, the clarity of insight remains.

331
00:23:52,095 --> 00:23:58,197
[SPEAKER_00]: When you have unwavering mindfulness of the mind, within the mind, you don't forget yourself.

332
00:23:59,320 --> 00:24:09,046
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether standing, walking, sitting or lying down, the awareness within makes it impossible to lose mindfulness.

333
00:24:09,126 --> 00:24:11,787
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a state of awareness that prevents you forgetting yourself.

334
00:24:12,648 --> 00:24:19,952
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness has become so strong that it is self-sustaining to the point where it becomes natural for the mind to be that way.

335
00:24:20,352 --> 00:24:21,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Or as they say, effortless.

336
00:24:21,873 --> 00:24:22,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Effortless.

337
00:24:23,334 --> 00:24:28,917
[SPEAKER_00]: These are the results of training and cultivating the mind and it is here where you go beyond doubt.

338
00:24:29,728 --> 00:24:31,329
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no doubts about the future.

339
00:24:31,529 --> 00:24:32,949
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no doubts about the past.

340
00:24:33,670 --> 00:24:37,672
[SPEAKER_00]: And accordingly, have no need to doubt about the present either.

341
00:24:38,492 --> 00:24:42,514
[SPEAKER_00]: You still have awareness that there is such a thing as past, present and future.

342
00:24:43,214 --> 00:24:44,775
[SPEAKER_00]: You are aware of the existence of time.

343
00:24:45,495 --> 00:24:48,837
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the reality of the past, present and future.

344
00:24:49,257 --> 00:24:52,178
[SPEAKER_00]: But you are no longer concerned, worried about it.

345
00:24:53,139 --> 00:24:55,800
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I keep saying it's real as such.

346
00:24:56,847 --> 00:25:00,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, people would say, oh, you must stop thinking about, stop planning about the future.

347
00:25:00,668 --> 00:25:01,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, why?

348
00:25:01,268 --> 00:25:02,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Time is just a construction.

349
00:25:03,269 --> 00:25:03,689
[SPEAKER_01]: So what?

350
00:25:03,809 --> 00:25:04,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it is.

351
00:25:04,669 --> 00:25:05,649
[SPEAKER_01]: It's real as such.

352
00:25:05,830 --> 00:25:06,990
[SPEAKER_01]: It's real as a construction.

353
00:25:07,990 --> 00:25:09,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Your past is real as such in the present.

354
00:25:10,391 --> 00:25:12,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Your future plans are real as such in the present.

355
00:25:15,192 --> 00:25:21,494
[SPEAKER_01]: You, you saying, you must not worry about the future, is you worrying about the future while you're saying you should not worry about the future.

356
00:25:23,087 --> 00:25:28,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're clearly saying, I must not plan the future, and you're planning to do that, to not plan the future.

357
00:25:28,751 --> 00:25:31,673
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're contradicting yourself within that same sentence.

358
00:25:33,775 --> 00:25:34,615
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you're wrong.

359
00:25:36,437 --> 00:25:44,522
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you realize, well, whether you think about the past or you think about the future, you cannot do that any differently than in the present.

360
00:25:46,063 --> 00:25:52,348
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're aware, if you're mindful of that inescapable,

361
00:25:53,175 --> 00:25:54,616
[SPEAKER_01]: To let go of the past, let go of the future.

362
00:25:55,757 --> 00:26:01,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can do that only on the basis of the presently enduring experience of whatever that experience is.

363
00:26:28,386 --> 00:26:30,307
[SPEAKER_00]: And this unwavering mindfulness?

364
00:26:30,808 --> 00:26:32,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that would be the unwavering mindfulness.

365
00:26:32,829 --> 00:26:39,654
[SPEAKER_01]: The mindfulness that doesn't chase the content of your past, the content of the future, or tries to get super absorbed in the content of the present.

366
00:26:42,516 --> 00:26:48,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead, it recognizes, well, whether I want this to be or not, it is.

367
00:26:48,380 --> 00:26:49,881
[SPEAKER_01]: It has a reason and it's persisting.

368
00:26:53,268 --> 00:27:03,414
[SPEAKER_01]: Any movement I make in regard to it, whether towards it, away from it, towards the past, towards the future, it's only on the basis of this thing still be... of this experience still being there.

369
00:27:03,654 --> 00:27:04,554
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm not saying that.

370
00:27:05,895 --> 00:27:15,240
[SPEAKER_01]: You start being mindful of that, that fundamental peripheral context that, oh, all my movements and motions and plans and, like,

371
00:27:16,044 --> 00:27:21,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentions, feelings, even perceptions in that manner, are on the basis of this thing still being there.

372
00:27:22,045 --> 00:27:24,066
[SPEAKER_01]: This experience still enduring on its own.

373
00:27:25,266 --> 00:27:28,527
[SPEAKER_01]: You start discerning that, you start developing mindfulness on the right level.

374
00:27:30,507 --> 00:27:31,207
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's effortless.

375
00:27:31,247 --> 00:27:37,729
[SPEAKER_01]: That is unwavering, because it doesn't... the context doesn't waver in regard to its content.

376
00:27:38,409 --> 00:27:39,129
[SPEAKER_01]: It's other way around.

377
00:27:39,429 --> 00:27:42,210
[SPEAKER_01]: The content gets its meaning from the context.

378
00:27:45,633 --> 00:27:48,495
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I think, you know, there's a difference between mindfulness and awareness.

379
00:27:49,955 --> 00:27:53,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's, it's, it's, it's, it comes together, mindfulness and awareness.

380
00:27:54,058 --> 00:27:55,018
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't separate it.

381
00:27:56,299 --> 00:28:05,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you could think of awareness as being more acutely mindful, and mindfulness as being mindful through and through, whether acutely or broadly, it doesn't matter.

382
00:28:05,904 --> 00:28:06,504
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still there.

383
00:28:07,105 --> 00:28:09,626
[SPEAKER_01]: So, mindfulness and awareness, it goes hand in hand.

384
00:28:11,227 --> 00:28:12,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Sati, sampajañña.

385
00:28:12,128 --> 00:28:12,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

386
00:28:14,490 --> 00:28:32,702
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in practical terms, I guess, like, I don't know, I can take an example of... Well, say like this, you, you, you, like the future example we gave, like you're worrying about something that's going to happen in two or three days, but your worry is right in the present.

387
00:28:35,363 --> 00:28:40,066
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to wait for three days to experience the worry on account of a future prospect.

388
00:28:40,847 --> 00:28:42,368
[SPEAKER_01]: You're experiencing it right here, right now.

389
00:28:43,794 --> 00:28:45,655
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to the future prospect.

390
00:28:46,815 --> 00:28:49,817
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as such, that future prospect is real.

391
00:28:50,377 --> 00:28:50,957
[SPEAKER_01]: As future.

392
00:28:51,498 --> 00:28:53,078
[SPEAKER_01]: That is present to you as such.

393
00:28:53,899 --> 00:28:56,280
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you can be concerned about the future.

394
00:28:56,700 --> 00:28:58,801
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can still grieve over the past.

395
00:29:01,542 --> 00:29:03,263
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't escape it, in other words.

396
00:29:03,283 --> 00:29:03,883
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I'm saying.

397
00:29:04,384 --> 00:29:09,926
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're looking at the content of your future or content of the past, you're bound by it in the present.

398
00:29:10,226 --> 00:29:11,427
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have no choice about that.

399
00:29:12,188 --> 00:29:15,470
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're looking at the content of your present, you're bound by it in the present.

400
00:29:15,490 --> 00:29:17,231
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize that was never the problem.

401
00:29:17,731 --> 00:29:34,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that happened to you in the past, the content of experiences that happened to you, good or bad, traumas or blessings, whatever, the content of the future prospect that might happen to you, some of it will happen to you, you have no say in that.

402
00:29:34,501 --> 00:29:35,842
[SPEAKER_01]: And that was never the problem.

403
00:29:36,242 --> 00:29:39,804
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem was that in the present, you were exposed to suffering.

404
00:29:40,643 --> 00:29:43,364
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even the future dukkha is presently felt.

405
00:29:44,204 --> 00:29:46,885
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from that point of view, dukkha is always in the present.

406
00:29:48,305 --> 00:29:52,646
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when you think about a future pain, it's painful right now.

407
00:29:53,867 --> 00:29:55,327
[SPEAKER_01]: It touches you right now.

408
00:29:55,747 --> 00:29:56,427
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

409
00:29:56,767 --> 00:30:01,749
[SPEAKER_01]: You presently can free yourself from liability to future suffering.

410
00:30:02,769 --> 00:30:06,710
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're actually waiting for the future content, then you can't free yourself right here and now.

411
00:30:08,953 --> 00:30:10,674
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that has presently manifested.

412
00:30:12,674 --> 00:30:16,496
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you understand it on that level, that that's where the problem is.

413
00:30:16,536 --> 00:30:20,317
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the problem is not in two or three days what's going to happen to you.

414
00:30:20,497 --> 00:30:22,278
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is that you are affected by it now.

415
00:30:23,138 --> 00:30:29,720
[SPEAKER_01]: If you find a way to not be affected by it now, you won't worry about it in three days' time either.

416
00:30:29,880 --> 00:30:37,463
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like that, say, meta-meditation, where you imagine meeting

417
00:30:38,017 --> 00:30:38,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

418
00:30:38,297 --> 00:30:49,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's it's along the right lines, but it's not quite accurate, because you need to know what, like, the problem is not the person, the problem is not meeting them or not meeting them.

419
00:31:04,050 --> 00:31:09,992
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is, you are trying to presently, right here, right now, get rid of the unpleasant feeling.

420
00:31:10,312 --> 00:31:11,592
[SPEAKER_01]: That's always the root of the problem.

421
00:31:12,272 --> 00:31:16,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Resistance to the displeasure, craving for pleasure.

422
00:31:17,994 --> 00:31:24,436
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you think about the future prospect, meeting somebody in three days, that always upsets you and hurts you,

423
00:31:26,628 --> 00:31:29,851
[SPEAKER_01]: That thing touches you, not because you meet that person, or you will meet it.

424
00:31:30,311 --> 00:31:34,574
[SPEAKER_01]: That thing touches you because you're trying to get rid of that prospect right here, right now.

425
00:31:34,794 --> 00:31:36,035
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you suffer.

426
00:31:36,916 --> 00:31:39,198
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why four noble truths are always in the present.

427
00:31:39,318 --> 00:31:40,359
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the present insight.

428
00:31:40,519 --> 00:31:42,060
[SPEAKER_01]: One insight of craving.

429
00:31:42,120 --> 00:31:44,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of craving means presence of suffering.

430
00:31:44,362 --> 00:31:46,503
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I suffer now, means I crave now.

431
00:31:47,584 --> 00:31:50,907
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't crave in three days time, or I don't crave in ten years ago.

432
00:31:51,847 --> 00:31:55,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, I've done it back then, and I probably will do it in the future, if I don't become an arahant.

433
00:31:55,583 --> 00:31:57,103
[SPEAKER_01]: But craving is always in the present.

434
00:31:57,363 --> 00:31:58,904
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why suffering is always in the present.

435
00:32:01,325 --> 00:32:07,046
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why if you approve that, none of your past changes, and none of your future needs to change either.

436
00:32:07,626 --> 00:32:09,627
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's why you can't worry on account of it.

437
00:32:11,247 --> 00:32:20,029
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not that you don't worry about the future or grieve over past, because you're so focused hard on the present moment that nothing else exists.

438
00:32:20,690 --> 00:32:22,010
[SPEAKER_01]: That's usually what people hope to do.

439
00:32:22,812 --> 00:32:28,136
[SPEAKER_01]: I focus, focus, focus, still I narrow my field of vision, so I don't see anything before or after.

440
00:32:29,417 --> 00:32:35,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to see that no matter how much you narrow it, before and after are always implied in the present moment.

441
00:32:35,642 --> 00:32:40,466
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether your present moment is narrow or wide, there is past, there is future within it.

442
00:32:42,287 --> 00:32:46,691
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're just trying to shut it down by trying to narrow on the content of whatever's in front of you.

443
00:32:47,575 --> 00:32:49,316
[SPEAKER_01]: That's usually what people think meditation is.

444
00:32:49,536 --> 00:32:57,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Focusing, focusing, focusing, focusing, so I kind of lose the sight of my past problems and don't overthink future issues.

445
00:32:59,320 --> 00:33:05,263
[SPEAKER_01]: But once you realize that it doesn't matter if you... if you still think about the past or if you're overthinking the future.

446
00:33:05,843 --> 00:33:07,404
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is never in those things.

447
00:33:07,464 --> 00:33:16,588
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is in you, currently, right here, right now, persisting in an attitude of not wanting disagreeable.

448
00:33:17,332 --> 00:33:18,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Be it past, be it future, be it present.

449
00:33:19,593 --> 00:33:22,734
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove the not wanting of disagreeable, that doesn't matter.

450
00:33:23,134 --> 00:33:25,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, in three days, I will meet this disagreeable person.

451
00:33:26,935 --> 00:33:33,537
[SPEAKER_01]: But I'm not trying to get rid of the disagreeability of that future prospect right now.

452
00:33:33,597 --> 00:33:35,297
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't matter what future awaits?

453
00:33:35,317 --> 00:33:36,498
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter what I do.

454
00:33:36,518 --> 00:33:37,738
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter what I say.

455
00:33:37,878 --> 00:33:39,199
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to plan my actions.

456
00:33:39,219 --> 00:33:40,899
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to rehearse my behavior.

457
00:33:40,999 --> 00:33:45,140
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to go through psychotherapy counseling sessions to plan how I will act in the future.

458
00:33:45,981 --> 00:33:46,701
[SPEAKER_01]: All I need to do

459
00:33:47,389 --> 00:33:52,311
[SPEAKER_01]: is not crave presently to get rid of the equally present prospect of disagreeable.

460
00:33:54,472 --> 00:33:56,793
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how future is in the present already.

461
00:33:57,113 --> 00:33:58,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Past is in the present.

462
00:33:58,954 --> 00:34:02,036
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why previous issues, they used to bother you.

463
00:34:02,056 --> 00:34:05,097
[SPEAKER_01]: You used to grieve over things that happened in the past.

464
00:34:05,117 --> 00:34:07,218
[SPEAKER_01]: You kept grieving over it for years.

465
00:34:07,258 --> 00:34:07,918
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will stop.

466
00:34:08,338 --> 00:34:09,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Because grieving is always in the present.

467
00:34:10,920 --> 00:34:13,901
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, literally, it doesn't matter what happened to you in the past.

468
00:34:14,321 --> 00:34:16,122
[SPEAKER_01]: You will be unable to grieve over it.

469
00:34:16,735 --> 00:34:28,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the examples in the suttas of that lady whose husband died, children got one drowned, the other killed by jackals, reached the village, parents, earthquake, roof fell, killed them.

470
00:34:28,758 --> 00:34:30,779
[SPEAKER_01]: She lost everyone in a course of one night.

471
00:34:31,899 --> 00:34:33,520
[SPEAKER_01]: And she went mad, basically, exactly.

472
00:34:33,540 --> 00:34:38,281
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's like the epitome of the traumatized experience you can imagine.

473
00:34:40,242 --> 00:34:44,263
[SPEAKER_01]: And she met the Buddha, got the teaching, practiced, became an arahant.

474
00:34:44,603 --> 00:34:45,363
[SPEAKER_01]: None of that changed.

475
00:34:46,094 --> 00:34:47,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Her loss hasn't changed.

476
00:34:48,375 --> 00:34:49,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Her previous griefs haven't changed.

477
00:34:50,236 --> 00:34:54,259
[SPEAKER_01]: But she cannot grieve over it anymore presently, which is always where the suffering was.

478
00:35:00,563 --> 00:35:03,044
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, it comes to that simile again, being an island.

479
00:35:03,344 --> 00:35:04,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Being an island, yep.

480
00:35:04,265 --> 00:35:09,548
[SPEAKER_00]: What future, what waves come, memories of the past, doesn't matter.

481
00:35:09,868 --> 00:35:14,491
[SPEAKER_01]: It's only your lack of clarity in regard to that, that sort of makes you affected by it.

482
00:35:16,044 --> 00:35:18,202
[SPEAKER_01]: Not discerning truly what an island is.

